WEBVTT

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:04.034
Welcome to this Java tutorial on reading input,

00:00:04.034 --> 00:00:07.249
where you'll create a text reader, choose line

00:00:07.249 --> 00:00:10.393
and numeric methods, trace each read, and use

00:00:10.393 --> 00:00:13.608
the returned values to calculate and display a

00:00:13.608 --> 00:00:14.097
result.

00:00:14.744 --> 00:00:17.823
Reading input lets the same program work with

00:00:17.823 --> 00:00:20.696
changing information, such as names, order

00:00:20.696 --> 00:00:22.680
quantities, and measurements.

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:25.759
A clear input format helps software interpret

00:00:25.759 --> 00:00:28.906
each piece correctly, so a calculation can use

00:00:28.906 --> 00:00:31.916
numbers instead of simply joining their text

00:00:31.916 --> 00:00:32.669
characters.

00:00:33.319 --> 00:00:36.233
A campus workshop needs receipts for supply

00:00:36.233 --> 00:00:36.572
kits.

00:00:36.572 --> 00:00:39.215
Each record gives an attendee's name, a

00:00:39.215 --> 00:00:41.994
whole-number kit quantity, and a price in

00:00:41.994 --> 00:00:44.094
dollars per kit, in that order.

00:00:44.094 --> 00:00:47.009
We'll use complete records with dot-decimal

00:00:47.009 --> 00:00:49.109
prices, such as one point five.

00:00:49.109 --> 00:00:52.227
The program calculates a cost; it doesn't take

00:00:52.227 --> 00:00:52.769
payment.

00:00:53.419 --> 00:00:56.242
Our goal is one receipt line containing the

00:00:56.242 --> 00:00:58.605
attendee's name and the amount owed.

00:00:58.605 --> 00:01:01.756
We'll supply repeatable text inside the program,

00:01:01.756 --> 00:01:04.776
so this demonstration won't wait for anyone to

00:01:04.776 --> 00:01:05.695
type an order.

00:01:05.695 --> 00:01:08.846
First we'll work through a practice record, then

00:01:08.846 --> 00:01:11.144
you'll predict a different receipt.

00:01:11.186 --> 00:01:14.373
Let's open Main.java in the Workspace and give

00:01:14.373 --> 00:01:17.699
the workshop program a place to read and process

00:01:17.699 --> 00:01:18.461
its record.

00:01:22.191 --> 00:01:25.102
Scanner is Java's ready-made text reader.

00:01:25.102 --> 00:01:28.226
An import lets this file use the short class

00:01:28.226 --> 00:01:31.350
name, Scanner; it doesn't create a reader or

00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:32.344
read any data.

00:01:32.344 --> 00:01:35.681
We'll put the import above Main, the class that

00:01:35.681 --> 00:01:36.959
holds our program.

00:01:36.959 --> 00:01:39.941
Its main method is where execution starts.

00:01:47.382 --> 00:01:48.937
The structure is ready.

00:01:48.937 --> 00:01:51.979
A reader needs an input source, the place its

00:01:51.979 --> 00:01:53.060
data comes from.

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:56.237
Our practice source gives Nora four kits at one

00:01:56.237 --> 00:01:58.130
dollar and fifty cents each.

00:01:58.130 --> 00:02:01.374
It also includes two spaces before and after her

00:02:01.374 --> 00:02:04.416
name, so we can check the cleanup you learned

00:02:04.416 --> 00:02:04.957
earlier.

00:02:10.483 --> 00:02:13.595
The keyword new creates a Scanner connected to

00:02:13.595 --> 00:02:14.407
that String.

00:02:14.407 --> 00:02:17.654
The newline escapes are source notation for line

00:02:17.654 --> 00:02:20.698
breaks, so the reader receives three separate

00:02:20.698 --> 00:02:23.065
lines, not backslashes and letters.

00:02:23.065 --> 00:02:25.027
It starts before Nora's name.

00:02:25.027 --> 00:02:28.071
Let's read that whole name line and store its

00:02:28.071 --> 00:02:30.033
cleaned text for the receipt.

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:36.671
NextLine returns the name without the line

00:02:36.671 --> 00:02:38.821
ending, but keeps the outside spaces.

00:02:38.821 --> 00:02:41.553
Trim then operates on that returned String, and

00:02:41.553 --> 00:02:43.819
buyer stores Nora without those spaces.

00:02:43.819 --> 00:02:45.969
The reader has moved to the quantity.

00:02:45.969 --> 00:02:48.061
For a kit count, we need an integer.

00:02:48.061 --> 00:02:50.270
Parsing turns text into a typed value.

00:02:50.270 --> 00:02:52.885
We'll parse the next token: the piece of text

00:02:52.885 --> 00:02:55.675
between whitespace separators such as spaces and

00:02:55.675 --> 00:02:56.430
line endings.

00:03:00.252 --> 00:03:03.477
NextInt turns the quantity text into the integer

00:03:03.477 --> 00:03:06.433
four and stops before its following newline.

00:03:06.433 --> 00:03:09.389
The price read will skip that whitespace and

00:03:09.389 --> 00:03:11.606
parse the next token as a double.

00:03:11.606 --> 00:03:14.831
NextInt cannot parse a decimal token such as one

00:03:14.831 --> 00:03:17.854
point five; a double receiving variable would

00:03:17.854 --> 00:03:19.802
not change that method's job.

00:03:24.216 --> 00:03:27.586
ItemPrice now holds the numeric value one point

00:03:27.586 --> 00:03:27.944
five.

00:03:27.944 --> 00:03:31.027
We can multiply four kits by one dollar and

00:03:31.027 --> 00:03:33.823
fifty cents per kit to get six dollars.

00:03:33.823 --> 00:03:36.834
Let's store that cost, join it with Nora's

00:03:36.834 --> 00:03:40.276
cleaned name and the word owes, and complete the

00:03:40.276 --> 00:03:41.566
program structure.

00:03:54.190 --> 00:03:57.002
The practice program now reads, cleans,

00:03:57.002 --> 00:03:58.661
calculates, and prints.

00:03:58.661 --> 00:04:01.978
Let's click Run to check the receipt made from

00:04:01.978 --> 00:04:03.565
those returned values.

00:04:08.936 --> 00:04:11.818
The receipt says Nora owes six point zero.

00:04:11.818 --> 00:04:14.701
The name has no outside padding because we

00:04:14.701 --> 00:04:16.485
stored the trimmed result.

00:04:16.485 --> 00:04:19.779
The amount comes from multiplying the two parsed

00:04:19.779 --> 00:04:20.328
numbers.

00:04:20.328 --> 00:04:23.417
Each read continued from the reader's current

00:04:23.417 --> 00:04:26.711
position; none of them started the source again.

00:04:26.804 --> 00:04:29.812
That reader position also explains a common

00:04:29.812 --> 00:04:30.442
surprise.

00:04:30.442 --> 00:04:33.590
Let's replace the receipt instructions with a

00:04:33.590 --> 00:04:36.808
small record that puts a count of three before

00:04:36.808 --> 00:04:37.787
the name Maya.

00:04:37.787 --> 00:04:41.145
We'll read the number first and inspect what the

00:04:41.145 --> 00:04:43.104
following line reads return.

00:04:52.043 --> 00:04:54.868
The numeric read takes three, but leaves the

00:04:54.868 --> 00:04:56.987
reader before that line's ending.

00:04:56.987 --> 00:05:00.068
A line read now takes the remaining text on this

00:05:00.068 --> 00:05:02.957
same line, which is empty in this source, and

00:05:02.957 --> 00:05:04.370
moves past the ending.

00:05:04.370 --> 00:05:07.323
A second line read can then take Maya from the

00:05:07.323 --> 00:05:08.286
following line.

00:05:08.286 --> 00:05:11.368
We'll store both results so we can compare them.

00:05:18.919 --> 00:05:22.256
Remainder holds empty text, and name holds Maya.

00:05:22.256 --> 00:05:25.384
Empty text is hard to see by itself, so we'll

00:05:25.384 --> 00:05:28.096
print square brackets around remainder.

00:05:28.096 --> 00:05:31.363
We'll also print the count and name on separate

00:05:31.363 --> 00:05:34.144
lines to make the reading order visible.

00:05:43.735 --> 00:05:46.818
Let's click Run and check the three stored

00:05:46.818 --> 00:05:47.405
results.

00:05:47.405 --> 00:05:50.709
The brackets will help us recognize the empty

00:05:50.709 --> 00:05:51.810
line remainder.

00:05:56.237 --> 00:05:59.062
We see three, an empty pair of brackets, and

00:05:59.062 --> 00:05:59.383
Maya.

00:05:59.383 --> 00:06:02.144
The first line call consumed only the empty

00:06:02.144 --> 00:06:04.071
remainder and its line ending.

00:06:04.071 --> 00:06:05.997
The next one reached the name.

00:06:05.997 --> 00:06:08.822
If text had followed the number on its line,

00:06:08.822 --> 00:06:11.840
that first call would return that text instead.

00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:14.601
Choose reads from the actual source layout;

00:06:14.601 --> 00:06:17.362
don't add an extra line read automatically.

00:06:17.474 --> 00:06:20.401
With the read order explained, let's return to

00:06:20.401 --> 00:06:21.737
the workshop receipt.

00:06:21.737 --> 00:06:24.791
We'll replace this small check with the workshop

00:06:24.791 --> 00:06:27.846
record: Maya requests three kits at four dollars

00:06:27.846 --> 00:06:29.373
and fifty cents per kit.

00:06:29.373 --> 00:06:32.427
Her name has no outside padding, so this version

00:06:32.427 --> 00:06:34.018
stores the line directly.

00:06:34.018 --> 00:06:36.945
A newly created reader starts at the beginning

00:06:36.945 --> 00:06:37.899
of this record.

00:06:48.138 --> 00:06:51.423
Customer receives Maya, and the reader passes

00:06:51.423 --> 00:06:53.103
the name line's ending.

00:06:53.103 --> 00:06:55.950
Quantity comes next, followed by price.

00:06:55.950 --> 00:06:59.309
Let's use the integer and double reads in that

00:06:59.309 --> 00:07:02.813
order, matching each value to its intended type.

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:14.436
The numeric calls turn the quantity and price

00:07:14.436 --> 00:07:17.257
text into values the calculation can use.

00:07:17.257 --> 00:07:20.490
The price read skips the newline left after the

00:07:20.490 --> 00:07:20.903
count.

00:07:20.903 --> 00:07:24.136
We'll multiply the stored numbers and build one

00:07:24.136 --> 00:07:27.301
receipt line from the customer name, the words

00:07:27.301 --> 00:07:29.640
and spaces in owes, and the total.

00:07:39.465 --> 00:07:42.624
Before we run Maya's program, write the exact

00:07:42.624 --> 00:07:44.308
receipt line you expect.

00:07:44.308 --> 00:07:47.467
Trace the returned name, quantity, and price,

00:07:47.467 --> 00:07:50.696
then calculate the cost and include the spaces

00:07:50.696 --> 00:07:52.661
supplied by the output text.

00:07:52.661 --> 00:07:55.258
Use Nora's worked receipt as a model.

00:07:55.258 --> 00:07:57.715
Pause here if you'd like more time.

00:08:05.791 --> 00:08:08.614
Let's click Run and compare the receipt with

00:08:08.614 --> 00:08:09.641
your prediction.

00:08:14.035 --> 00:08:16.966
The program prints Maya owes thirteen point

00:08:16.966 --> 00:08:17.306
five.

00:08:17.306 --> 00:08:20.509
Three kits at four dollars and fifty cents each

00:08:20.509 --> 00:08:23.099
cost thirteen dollars and fifty cents.

00:08:23.099 --> 00:08:26.098
The printed double uses one fractional digit

00:08:26.098 --> 00:08:26.439
here.

00:08:26.439 --> 00:08:29.028
Compare that name and amount with your

00:08:29.028 --> 00:08:29.778
prediction.

00:08:29.778 --> 00:08:32.709
The program used the values returned by the

00:08:32.709 --> 00:08:34.685
reads to produce the receipt.

00:08:34.799 --> 00:08:37.469
These examples didn't wait for typing because

00:08:37.469 --> 00:08:39.426
their input was already supplied.

00:08:39.426 --> 00:08:42.215
Java's standard input stream, called System.in,

00:08:42.215 --> 00:08:44.706
is another possible source; an interactive

00:08:44.706 --> 00:08:47.138
program can read typed responses from it.

00:08:47.138 --> 00:08:49.571
Keep the supplied text for this exercise.

00:08:49.571 --> 00:08:52.181
Running the complete program again creates a

00:08:52.181 --> 00:08:54.613
fresh reader, while repeating a read on a

00:08:54.613 --> 00:08:56.749
consumed reader does not restart it.

00:08:56.825 --> 00:08:59.750
We've connected input to the workshop's result.

00:08:59.750 --> 00:09:02.178
The import made the reader's class name

00:09:02.178 --> 00:09:04.854
available, and new created a reader for the

00:09:04.854 --> 00:09:05.850
supplied source.

00:09:05.850 --> 00:09:08.091
Newline escapes organized that text.

00:09:08.091 --> 00:09:11.078
Matching line and numeric reads advanced through

00:09:11.078 --> 00:09:13.692
it, while parsing produced usable numbers.

00:09:13.692 --> 00:09:16.431
Your earlier text and arithmetic skills then

00:09:16.431 --> 00:09:17.800
assembled the receipt.

00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:20.902
Now build the notebook's Luis receipt.

00:09:20.902 --> 00:09:23.848
Use its exact padded source and variable names,

00:09:23.848 --> 00:09:26.857
read the name and numbers, and store the cleaned

00:09:26.857 --> 00:09:29.803
name before making the receipt for five kits at

00:09:29.803 --> 00:09:31.872
two dollars and fifty cents each.

00:09:31.872 --> 00:09:34.630
After the first run, predict a change to six

00:09:34.630 --> 00:09:36.635
kits, test it, and restore five.

00:09:36.635 --> 00:09:39.394
Explain why each complete run starts a fresh

00:09:39.394 --> 00:09:39.895
reading.
